         Project Name - terraform-aws-vpc-ec2-with-variables
COMPLETE STEP-BY-STEP TERRAFORM DOCUMENTATION (VPC + EC2)
 Project Goal
Create AWS Infrastructure using Terraform:
•	VPC
•	Public Subnet
•	Private Subnet
•	Internet Gateway
•	Route Tables
•	Security Group
•	EC2 Instance (Free Tier)
•	Variables (string, number, bool, list, tuple, map, object, any)
•	Dynamic Tags
Region: us-west-1
________________________________________
 STEP 1: Launch EC2 (Terraform Server)
1.	Go to AWS Console
2.	Open EC2 → Launch Instance
3.	Select:
o	AMI: Amazon Linux 2
o	Instance Type: t3.micro (Free Tier)
4.	Create Key Pair
5.	Allow SSH (Port 22)
6.	Launch Instance
7.	Connect via SSH:
ssh -i key.pem ec2-user@your-public-ip
________________________________________
 STEP 2: Install Terraform on EC2
Run these commands:
sudo yum update -y
sudo yum install -y yum-utils
sudo yum-config-manager --add-repo https://rpm.releases.hashicorp.com/AmazonLinux/hashicorp.repo
sudo yum -y install terraform
Check version:
terraform -v
________________________________________
STEP 3: Create Project Folder
mkdir terraform-project
cd terraform-project
________________________________________
 STEP 4: Create Required Files (Best Practice Structure)
Create files:
touch provider.tf
touch variables.tf
touch vpc.tf
touch ec2.tf
Project structure:
terraform-project/
│
├── provider.tf
├── variables.tf
├── vpc.tf
└── ec2.tf
________________________________________
 STEP 5: Provider Configuration (provider.tf)
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-1"
}
 ________________________________________
STEP 6: Variables File (variables.tf)
Includes all types: string, number, bool, list, tuple, map, object, any + tags
variable "aws_region" {
  type    = string
  default = "us-west-1"
}

variable "vpc_name" {
  type    = string
  default = "vpc-1"
}

variable "ssh_port" {
  type    = number
  default = 22
}

variable "associate_public_ip" {
  type    = bool
  default = true
}

variable "private_subnet_cidr" {
  type    = list(string)
  default = ["10.0.4.0/23"]
}

variable "instance_config" {
  type    = tuple([string, string])
  default = ["us-west-1a", "t3.micro"]
}

variable "common_tags" {
  type = map(string)
  default = {
    Owner   = "Shivani"
    Project = "Terraform-VPC-EC2"
    Env     = "Dev"
  }
}

variable "ec2_config" {
  type = object({
    ami           = string
    instance_type = string
    name          = string
  })

  default = {
    ami           = "ami-0d53d72369335a9d6"
    instance_type = "t3.micro"
    name          = "shivani-inst"
  }
}

variable "tags" {
  description = "Custom tag list"
  type        = any
  default     = ["shivani-resource", "Shivani", "Dev"]
}
 

 
________________________________________
 STEP 7: VPC Infrastructure (vpc.tf)
resource "aws_vpc" "my_vpc" {
  cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/21"

  tags = {
    Name  = var.vpc_name
    Env   = var.tags[2]
    Owner = var.tags[1]
  }
}

resource "aws_subnet" "pub_sub" {
  vpc_id            = aws_vpc.my_vpc.id
  cidr_block        = "10.0.0.0/22"
  availability_zone = var.instance_config[0]

  tags = {
    Name  = "${var.tags[0]}-public"
    Env   = var.tags[2]
    Owner = var.tags[1]
  }
}

resource "aws_subnet" "pri_sub" {
  vpc_id            = aws_vpc.my_vpc.id
  cidr_block        = var.private_subnet_cidr[0]
  availability_zone = "us-west-1c"

  tags = {
    Name  = "${var.tags[0]}-private"
    Env   = var.tags[2]
    Owner = var.tags[1]
  }
}

resource "aws_internet_gateway" "my_igw" {
  vpc_id = aws_vpc.my_vpc.id

  tags = {
    Name  = "${var.tags[0]}-igw"
    Env   = var.tags[2]
    Owner = var.tags[1]
  }
}

resource "aws_route_table" "my_rt" {
  vpc_id = aws_vpc.my_vpc.id

  tags = {
    Name  = "${var.tags[0]}-private-rt"
    Env   = var.tags[2]
    Owner = var.tags[1]
  }
}

resource "aws_route_table_association" "my_prta" {
  route_table_id = aws_route_table.my_rt.id
  subnet_id      = aws_subnet.pri_sub.id
}

resource "aws_route_table" "my_pubrt" {
  vpc_id = aws_vpc.my_vpc.id

  tags = {
    Name  = "${var.tags[0]}-public-rt"
    Env   = var.tags[2]
    Owner = var.tags[1]
  }
}

resource "aws_route" "public_route" {
  route_table_id         = aws_route_table.my_pubrt.id
  destination_cidr_block = "0.0.0.0/0"
  gateway_id             = aws_internet_gateway.my_igw.id
}

resource "aws_route_table_association" "my_pubrta" {
  route_table_id = aws_route_table.my_pubrt.id
  subnet_id      = aws_subnet.pub_sub.id
}

resource "aws_security_group" "my_sg" {
  name        = "my_sg_1"
  description = "allow ssh access"
  vpc_id      = aws_vpc.my_vpc.id

  ingress {
    from_port   = var.ssh_port
    to_port     = var.ssh_port
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  tags = {
    Name  = "${var.tags[0]}-sg"
    Env   = var.tags[2]
    Owner = var.tags[1]
  }
}
 
 

________________________________________
 STEP 8: EC2 Instance (ec2.tf)
resource "aws_instance" "inst_1" {
  ami                         = "ami-0d53d72369335a9d6"
  instance_type               = "t3.micro"   
  subnet_id                   = aws_subnet.pub_sub.id
  vpc_security_group_ids      = [aws_security_group.my_sg.id]
  associate_public_ip_address = true

  tags = {
    Name = "shivani-inst"
  }
}
 
________________________________________
 STEP 9: Initialize Terraform
terraform init
 
This downloads AWS provider plugins.
________________________________________
 STEP 10: Validate Configuration
terraform validate

 
Checks syntax errors (bool, duplicate resource, etc.)
________________________________________
STEP 11: Preview Infrastructure
terraform plan
Shows what resources will be created:
•	VPC
•	Subnets
•	IGW
•	Route Tables
•	Security Group
•	EC2
 
________________________________________
 STEP 12: Deploy Infrastructure
terraform apply
Type:
Yes
 
Now AWS resources will be created.
________________________________________
 STEP 13: Verify Resources
Check state:
terraform state list
Expected output:
aws_instance.inst_1
aws_internet_gateway.my_igw
aws_route.public_route
aws_route_table.my_pubrt
aws_route_table.my_rt
aws_route_table_association.my_prta
aws_route_table_association.my_pubrta
aws_security_group.my_sg
aws_subnet.pri_sub
aws_subnet.pub_sub
aws_vpc.my_vpc
 
________________________________________
 STEP 14: Check in AWS Console
Go to:
1.	AWS Console → VPC → Your VPC
 
2.	Subnets section
 

 
3.	Route tables - shivani-resource-private-rt
 
shivani-resource-public-rt

 
4. Internet gateways - shivani-resource-igw
 
4.	Security Groups 
 
5.	EC2 → Instances
Region must be us-west-1 (very important)
 


